WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.000
Hello, in today's video

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.800
I will show you how it was possible to execute commands on

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.700
GitHub server using a malicious YAML file.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.800
It allowed to steal all the source code stored on the server.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.900
The vulnerability was found and reported by William Bowling

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.500
and he got $25,000 for it.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:23.300
He also has more write-ups about hacking GitHub on his blog,

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:27.600
make sure to check that out. But for now enjoy the video!

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.800
Probably most of you already know what's GitHub.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.600
It's a place for storage and version control of the source code.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.900
GitHub pages is a service that allows to serve static

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.300
websites from GitHub repositories.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:49.900
It's available both in the global GitHub instance and in

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.800
GitHub Enterprise and the latter one was vulnerable to this

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:59.500
attack. Jekyll is a software used for generating static websites

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.599
that can be used on GitHub Pages. Static websites are the

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:07.900
ones that do not process user input.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.600
It could be just a set of HTML files, but then to each file

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.700
you would have to copy your header and footer, so it would

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:19.700
be better to use some kind of a template.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.500
And that's exactly what the Jekyll is used for - for creating

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.800
static websites from templates.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.100
It uses markdown to create those templates. And markdown is

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.500
a really simple language that allows creation of HTML websites.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.200
In its base version,

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.343
It looks like this: 

00:01:41.343 --> 00:01:43.343
And the amount of options is really limited.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.700
So there are extended versions of markdown and kramdown is

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:54.000
one of them. It gives the user more ability to style his website,

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.600
but most importantly for us, it also allows to specify some

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.400
options for the parser.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.900
It can be used in a few different ways,

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.400
but GitHub used it as a ruby library.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.600
Jekyll uses _config.yml file for storing configuration

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.700
options, including the ones for the kramdown parser.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:21.000
And while looking at the source code of the library, the

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.500
researcher noticed input option that was not described in

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:26.800
the documentation.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.900
The parser took this input option made the first letter capital

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.100
and called function try_require. And hat function created

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:44.500
a path kramdown/parser/:input tried to import

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:45.300
that file.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:50.500
The idea was to give the user the ability to use different

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:54.000
parsers from kramdown/parsers/ directory.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:59.200
However, this function did not forbid putting in ../

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.100
sequence in this input option, making it possible to

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.500
perform a directory traversal attack.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:12.100
The way Ruby import works is that it executes the file straight

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:17.800
away after importing, even before you call any function. It works

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:22.100
the same way in Python and that's why if __name__ == "__main__".

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.200
is commonly used there.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:29.500
That means that we can import arbitrary Ruby file from the

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.800
server and it will be executed.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.600
But first we need to upload our Ruby file onto the server

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.300
into the location known by us.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:45.500
And this is not a trivial task, because files from the repositories

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:50.400
are stored in hashed locations, which means that we cannot

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.000
use them because we cannot predict where they are.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:59.900
The researcher tested it on his own version of GitHub Enterprise,

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.300
which means that he had access to

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:08.700
the server and he used opensnoop tool to see what files are

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:13.400
being opened on the server and he noticed that before being

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:18.200
transferred to the hashed location, files are being put into

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.100
the temporary directory.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:24.800
But the path of the directory was fully predictable making

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:29.400
our attack more feasible. But those files were very quickly

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:32.500
deleted and you'd have to be very very lucky

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.500
to hit the import at the moment when they are still present.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:41.000
But in this kinda race condition, you can extend the time

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.700
window you have by creating files that will take more time

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:46.900
to be processed.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.000
And that's exactly what the researcher did.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:53.200
He created five 100 megabyte files.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:57.500
And in the infinite Loop pushed it along with the Ruby file.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.000
Then, after about a minute.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:05.300
He noticed that the Ruby file got executed. And by having

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:08.900
the ability to execute code on the server,

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:14.400
you can easily steal or information that's stored there.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.600
And after reporting the issue the researcher found yet another

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:22.700
set of options that let to the same kind of attack and reported

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:23.700
it as well.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.700
The vulnerability was fixed and right now,

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:30.600
the vulnerability is no longer exploitable in the newest

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.500
version of GitHub Enterprise.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:39.642
The reward for that was $20,000 plus 5,000 bonus.

00:05:39.877 --> 00:05:42.000
And that's it for today's video.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.900
Lately, there are a lot of vulnerabilities affecting YAML files,

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:49.300
so that is definitely something we should learn and if you

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.500
have learned something from this video leave a like and leave

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:55.800
a subscription if you haven't already. But for now, thanks

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.200
for watching and goodbye!

